{"id":"02d95bc6-02f0-408d-b958-58bd00cdb1ae","object":"chat.completion","created":1784808499,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    for key in ('text', 'passes', 'max_rounds'):\\n        if key not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required field: {key}\\\")\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    for i, p in enumerate(passes):\\n        for key in ('name', 'old', 'new'):\\n            if key not in p:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Pass {i} missing {key}\\\")\\n    artifact = text\\n    trace = []\\n    changed = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        round_changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            if p['old'] in artifact:\\n                artifact = artifact.replace(p['old'], p['new'], 1)\\n                trace.append(p['name'])\\n                round_changed = True\\n        if not round_changed:\\n            changed = False\\n            break\\n        else:\\n            changed = True\\n    fixed_point = not changed\\n    return {'artifact': artifact, 'trace': trace, 'fixed_point': fixed_point}\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the problem. It seems like a code reduction task: we have a text (artifact) and a list of passes. Each pass has an old and new string. We need to simulate applying passes in order, each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep (i.e., replace first occurrence of old with new). Then record passes that change. Repeat sweeps (rounds) until no change or max_rounds reached. Return artifact, trace (list of pass names that changed in order), and fixed_point flag (true if no change in last round). The input provides case with text, passes, max_rounds. We need to implement solve(case) that returns a JSON-serializable object, presumably with keys like \"artifact\", \"trace\", \"fixed_point\".\n\nBut note: the fixture only shows input shapes, not expected outputs. We need to infer the output structure. Probably like: {\"artifact\": \"...\", \"trace\": [\"halve-a\", ...], \"fixed_point\": bool}. Also need to handle multiple cases? solve takes a single case (the payload). Return object.\n\nWe must use only Python standard library. No mutation of case. Must validate required fields: text, passes (each with name, old, new), max_rounds. Possibly also ensure each pass has those fields. Return result.\n\nImplementation: define solve(case) that extracts text, passes list, max_rounds. Make a copy of text for artifact. Initialize trace empty. For round in range(max_rounds): changed = False; for each pass in passes: find first occurrence of pass['old'] in current artifact; if found, replace it with pass['new'], record pass name in trace, set changed=True; note: \"first eligible rewrite per sweep\" means we only do at most one replacement per pass per sweep (even if multiple occurrences? It says \"first eligible rewrite per sweep\" so yes, only one). After processing all passes, if not changed, break. After loop, fixed_point = not changed (or if stopped due to no change after a round). Actually if after a round no change, we break, so fixed_point = True. If we exhaust max_rounds, fixed_point = False (since we stopped due to budget, possibly not at fixed point). But need to check: if last round had changes but we run out of rounds, fixed_point false. If no changes at all, fixed_point true.\n\nEdge cases: text might be empty, passes might be empty. Also need to handle that passes might not have name? But treat as required.\n\nReturn dict with keys: \"artifact\" (final string), \"trace\" (list of pass names in order of application that changed), \"fixed_point\" (bool).\n\nAlso note: the case object may have _case_nonce, but we ignore.\n\nImplementation must be careful about string replacement: use str.replace(old, new, 1) to replace first occurrence.\n\nNow produce JSON output.\n\nWe must output only one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being Python source code as a JSON string. So we need to encode the Python code as a string with proper escaping.\n\nLet's write solve function.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate required fields\n    for key in ('text', 'passes', 'max_rounds'):\n        if key not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing required field: {key}\")\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    # Validate each pass has name, old, new\n    for i, p in enumerate(passes):\n        for key in ('name', 'old', 'new'):\n            if key not in p:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Pass {i} missing {key}\")\n    artifact = text\n    trace = []\n    changed = False\n    for round_num in range(max_rounds):\n        round_changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            if p['old'] in artifact:\n                # replace first occurrence\n                artifact = artifact.replace(p['old'], p['new'], 1)\n                trace.append(p['name'])\n                round_changed = True\n        if not round_changed:\n            changed = False\n            break\n        else:\n            changed = True\n    # After loop, fixed_point is True if we exited due to no change (i.e., not changed after final round)\n    fixed_point = not changed  # careful: If we broke due to no change, changed is False from that round? Actually we set changed = round_changed after each round, and if round_changed False, we set changed = False and break. So after loop, if we broke, changed is False. If we finished all rounds, changed is True (from last round). So fixed_point = not changed.\n    # But if max_rounds==0? Then loop doesn't run, changed stays False, fixed_point True? Probably okay.\n    return {\"artifact\": artifact, \"trace\": trace, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point}\n\nTest with given case: text \"aaaa bb cc\", passes: halve-a aa->a, drop-b bb->b, drop-c  cc->\"\". max_rounds=8.\n\nRound1: start \"aaaa bb cc\". halve-a: replace first \"aa\" with \"a\" -> \"aa bb cc\"? Actually \"aaaa\" has two \"aa\" overlapping? First occurrence is at position 0, replace with \"a\" -> \"aa bb cc\". Then drop-b: \"bb\" found -> \"aa b cc\". Then drop-c: \" cc\" found -> \"aa b\". So after round1: \"aa b\", trace: [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\"].\nRound2: halve-a: \"aa\" found -> \"a b\". drop-b: \"bb\" not found? \"a b\" has no \"bb\". drop-c: \" cc\" not found. So only halve-a changes. Trace now [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\",\"halve-a\"]. Artifact \"a b\".\nRound3: halve-a: \"aa\" not found, drop-b not, drop-c not. No change, break. Fixed_point True. Return artifact \"a b\", trace as above, fixed_point True.\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nNow encode as JSON string. We need to write the Python source as a string inside JSON. So we'll generate the code string, escape newlines (to \\n), backslashes, quotes. Then put it in {\"implementation\": \"<code>\"}.\n\nMake sure no leading/trailing spaces outside JSON. Use strict JSON.\n\nWe'll output the exact JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":879,"completion_tokens":1676,"total_tokens":2555,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":640},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1397},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":640,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":239},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}